{
  "paraId": "193B42C3",
  "style": "",
  "text": "Our study investigated the outcomes of lung cancer surgery in elderly patients, focusing on postoperative complications and mortality rates. We observed an in-hospital mortality rate of 1.7% after lung cancer surgery. Benker et al. [7] reported an overall operative mortality rate of 1.56%, similar to our finding. In contrast, Detillon et al. [8] found a higher operative mortality rate by 2.1% in patients ≥ 60 years with lung cancer resections compared to our data. Benker et al. [7] also reported an overall postoperative complication rate of 38.6% in 1219 patients who underwent resection for NSCLC, comprising pulmonary (26.4%), cardiac (14.8%), and gastrointestinal (3.0%) complications, and 78.9% of their patients had at least one comorbidity; these figures are of a similar magnitude to the 29.3% rate of Clavien–Dindo grade ≥ II complications in our cohort, although the two series differ in complication grading and case mix."
}

