  "text": "Advancing age is accompanied by a higher burden of comorbidity and by an increased risk of malnutrition-related mortality [23]. Malnutrition is a well-established risk factor for morbidity and mortality in surgical patients, and elderly patients typically present with a greater burden of surgical comorbidity than younger patients [24]. In a Japanese nationwide study of octogenarians, poor nutritional status, reflected by low serum albumin levels, independently predicted postoperative complications [25]. Okada et al. [10] reported that lower prognostic nutritional index (PNI) values were significantly associated with postoperative pulmonary complications in elderly patients with non-small cell lung cancer. In contrast, we found no association between either serum albumin levels or PNI values and postoperative complications in our cohort of patients aged ≥ 60 years. This discrepancy may be explained by incomplete laboratory data: serum albumin was not measured in all patients, and PNI therefore could not be calculated for the entire cohort, which may have limited our ability to detect a true association."
